          "evidence": "The title reads 'Top Screening Centers: Outreach Hours vs Referral Count,' and the subtitle reads 'Selected high-referral centers shown in dashboard summary.'",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The horizontal axis is labeled 'Monthly outreach hours' and its printed ticks increase from 30 at the left to 70 at the right; the vertical axis is labeled 'Referral count' and its printed ticks increase from 60 near the bottom to 160 near the top.",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "3": {
          "evidence": "Center A is the leftmost and lowest labeled point; Center B is to its right and higher; Center C is further right and higher; Center D is further right and higher; Center E is the rightmost and highest labeled point.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the horizontal axis encodes monthly outreach hours increasing left-to-right and the vertical axis encodes referral count increasing upward, then the labeled center points can be compared as an observed association. A consistent higher-hours/higher-referrals pattern among the displayed selected centers is suitable for broader network review, but this cross-sectional selected-center chart alone does not establish causality, network-wide representativeness, or grounds for immediate network-level advancement.",
        "evidence": "The axis labels and tick values confirm the coordinate system: 'Monthly outreach hours' increases left-to-right (30 to 70) and 'Referral count' increases upward (60 to 160). The positions of the labeled points (A to E) show a clear monotonic trend: as outreach hours increase from ~32 to ~71, referral count increases from ~61 to ~162. This establishes a positive association among the selected centers. The reading's condition — 'Applies only to the displayed highlighted/selected high-referral screening centers and assumes the axis titles describe the plotted coordinates.' — is met. The reading correctly states that this pattern is suitable for broader network review but does not establish causality or network-wide representativeness, which is accurate given the chart's scope. The reading does not claim the pattern is causal or representative of the entire network, which is a correct limitation.",
